  "reconstruction": {
    "scope": "Specific Po Nagar standing complex: four mapped brick shrine envelopes with tall four-register main tower/miniature crowns, pointed south pyramid, smaller northwest stepped crown/saddle cap and low southeast saddle cap, projecting pointed entry porches/stone lintels/real recessed doors, original simplified relief/leaf/corner ornament and short local approaches. Mapped lower mandapa is an unroofed platform with approximate twenty octagonal tapered brick pillars and narrow grade-following approach steps. Existing shared brick/stone/dark-recess materials; no copied deity/story/text atlas, full ritual interior, vanished temple reconstruction or broad upper-court slab.",
    "identityBasis": "Named standard PMTiles Po Nagar Cham Towers archaeological-site point35184518869179 correlates four tower buildings35184568984879..882 and lower mandapa rectangle35184568984883. No exact-QID building-footprint claim. Anchor109.19555556,12.26527778; heading0, internal local+x angle+.04rad; porches face approximately east/local+x. Actual mapped rectangles establish position and extent; body/porch subdivisions, miniatures, pillar stations and stair engineering are ordinary-picture/map approximations. Museum35184568984890 and all independent buildings remain native.",
    "heightBasis": "Ordinary-picture/map visualization estimates, not surveys: main footprint about14.6x12.8m including east porch, tall main body11.2m/four receding upper registers to22.7m; south body6m/pointed roof-finial15m. Northwest small body4.5m, stepped upper block/corner crowns and saddle cap to10m; southeast low body4.1m/upper saddle cap to7.7m. Main porch about7.6m pointed crest, south porch crest about6.6m and smaller entry crests about5m. Lower mandapa approximately22.5x12m, twenty picture-led pillar stations with taller inner4.7m and lower outer2.0m shafts. Hidden allocations, exact relief inventory, repair patterns and absolute elevations approximate.",
    "terrainBasis": "Original standard6m Mapzen hill/city ground and water retained. Each tower and occupied mandapa platform has its own local level top with grade-buried bottom; short tower approaches and narrow connecting stair/cheeks follow the sampled coarse native grade. Open upper courts stay native ground. No broad hill/precinct slab, flattening or ground/water cutout. Coarse DEM does not resolve actual paved terraces, stair thresholds or cliff/retaining faces. Its retained mandapa maximum sample is slightly above the main-tower floor, so the narrow approach follows coarse native grade rather than recreating the photographed steep descent. This level relationship remains a terrain-resolution limitation.",
    "architecturalAngleRadians": 0.04,
